Coastal wetlands, including marshes, mangroves, and swamps, act as natural sponges and shock absorbers for coastal communities. Their ability to mitigate flood damage during storms is not merely theoretical; it is a well-documented physical reality that relies on specific hydrodynamic and biological processes.

The primary mechanism at work is friction. When storm surges—walls of water pushed by high winds—move from the open ocean toward the shore, they encounter the dense vegetation of wetlands. Stems, roots, and leaves create significant drag against the moving water. This friction slows the surge down, reducing its speed